from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('4');add=A.add
add('2.1-01','A rule or tape measures length, while a graduated cylinder measures liquid volume.','Instrument choice depends on the quantity, its size and the resolution needed.',
'A long flexible distance is more conveniently measured with a tape than with repeated short ruler placements. A small liquid volume needs a suitably graduated vessel rather than a length scale alone. Read the graduation unit before recording a value and check the zero or reference point. A cylinder can have a large capacity yet still be unsuitable for a small change if its divisions are too wide. Name both instrument and technique in a measurement plan.',
'Explain why a ruler is suitable for a straight strip length but a measuring cylinder is suitable for a liquid volume.','The ruler’s scale directly gives a linear separation.~The cylinder is calibrated to the three-dimensional volume occupied by its liquid.')
add('2.1-01-M01','A measured length is the difference between the scale positions of its ends.','The scale should be aligned with the intended length and viewed without avoidable parallax.',
'An object beginning at 3.0 cm and ending at 11.6 cm is 8.6 cm long. Its length is not 11.6 cm unless its first end is at zero. Use a tape for longer distances and keep it appropriately straight without stretching it. For a curved perimeter, a flexible thread can transfer the path length to a straight rule, with additional uncertainty from following and transferring the curve.',
'Describe how to measure a straight strip whose ends are at the 1.5 cm and 9.2 cm positions on a ruler.','Read both positions and subtract 1.5 cm from 9.2 cm to obtain its length.')
add('2.1-01-M02','A measuring cylinder reports liquid volume from the level of its surface.','For water in a clean cylinder, the bottom of the curved meniscus is read at eye level.',
'Place the cylinder vertically on a level surface and allow the liquid to settle. Read the appropriate scale division with the eye level with the meniscus, rather than viewing from above. Record a value consistent with the scale resolution. For displacement measurements, subtract the original water volume from the volume after the object is immersed. Avoid trapped bubbles and ensure the object is fully submerged when that method is intended.',
'Describe one action needed for a reliable water-volume reading in a measuring cylinder.','Place the eye level with the bottom of the meniscus and read the cylinder scale there.')
add('2.1-02','A small dimension may require greater resolution than an ordinary ruler provides.','Vernier calipers and a micrometer use mechanical scales to resolve smaller changes, but their measuring faces suit different tasks.',
'Caliper outside jaws measure external widths, inside jaws can measure an opening and a depth probe can measure a recess. A conventional external micrometer encloses an object between an anvil and spindle; it is not a suitable instrument for an inside diameter. Check range and zero before measurement. Resolution describes the scale increment, not a guarantee that each measurement is accurate. Poor alignment or excessive measuring force can defeat the apparent precision.',
'Explain the difference between using caliper internal jaws and an external micrometer for a ring measurement.','Internal jaws can reach and separate the inside surfaces of the opening.~An external micrometer’s measuring faces instead enclose an outside thickness or diameter.')
add('2.1-02-M01','Repeated layers can magnify a thickness too small for a single ruler reading.','Dividing the total thickness by the number of similar layers estimates the thickness of one layer.',
'A stack of 80 similar sheets measuring 8.0 mm gives an average sheet thickness of 0.10 mm. Check that the sheets are similar and pressed together consistently without large gaps or excessive compression. Measuring a stack reduces the fractional influence of the ruler reading uncertainty compared with measuring one thin sheet. The result is an average, so it does not establish that every sheet has precisely the same thickness.',
'Describe a ruler method for estimating the thickness of one thin sheet of paper.','Measure a stack of many similar sheets and divide its total thickness by the number of sheets.')
add('2.1-02-M02','A vernier reading combines the main-scale position with the aligned vernier division.','The vernier resolution and zero correction must be known for the particular instrument.',
'For an instrument with 0.1 mm vernier resolution, a main-scale reading of 18 mm and an aligned sixth division gives 18.6 mm before a zero correction. Do not assume the same resolution for every vernier. Close the appropriate jaws gently and check that the measured surfaces are properly contacted. Read inside and outside dimensions using the correct jaw faces. Record a known zero error and apply the correction with the sign that removes its bias.',
'Describe how a main-scale and vernier-scale reading are combined when the vernier resolution is known.','Add the main-scale reading just before the vernier zero to the aligned vernier division multiplied by its resolution, then apply a justified zero correction.')
add('2.1-02-M03','A micrometer reading combines the sleeve reading with the thimble contribution.','For a 0.5 mm screw pitch divided into 50 thimble divisions, each division represents 0.01 mm.',
'If the sleeve shows 5.5 mm and the thimble contributes 0.23 mm, the reading is 5.73 mm before correction. Look for a visible half-millimetre sleeve mark; overlooking it produces a substantial error. Use the ratchet or suitable light measuring force rather than tightening hard against the object. Check the zero with the faces closed cleanly. A very thin or compressible sample can change thickness under measuring pressure, so technique remains important.',
'Describe one technique that avoids excessive force when measuring thickness with a micrometer.','Use the ratchet or specified light-force mechanism to bring the spindle into contact gently.')
add('2.1-03','A time interval is the elapsed duration between a defined start and finish.','A clock provides time readings, while a stopwatch can directly display elapsed time.',
'When two clock readings are used, subtract the starting time from the finishing time, accounting for a change of minute or hour. A stopwatch still needs a clearly defined event for starting and stopping. Choose a device with a resolution appropriate to the duration; the display’s last digit does not eliminate reaction-time uncertainty. For repeated motion, specify what counts as one complete cycle before timing it.',
'Explain the link between clock time readings and a stopwatch duration for the same event.','The difference between the final and initial clock readings gives elapsed time.~A correctly started stopwatch can display that duration directly.')
add('2.1-03-M01','An elapsed interval from a clock is found by subtracting the starting reading from the finishing reading.','Time notation must be handled as minutes and seconds rather than as a decimal fraction.',
'From 10:14:45 to 10:15:20 the interval is 35 s. The minute boundary does not mean that only the final seconds should be subtracted. Converting both readings to a common seconds count can make the subtraction clearer. A wristwatch may be sufficient for a long duration but unsuitable for a very short event. Define the start and end events so that the time being measured is reproducible.',
'Describe how to obtain the elapsed time from two clock readings.','Subtract the starting reading from the finishing reading, accounting for any minute or hour rollover.')
add('2.1-03-M02','A stopwatch measures elapsed time between its start and stop actions.','Human reaction-time variation can be important when the physical event is short.',
'Check that the stopwatch is reset or note its starting indication before a run. Use an observable event such as a bob crossing a reference line in a specified direction. When several repeats are possible, record each rather than replacing them by a remembered average. Timing a longer repeated sequence can reduce the relative effect of starting and stopping delays. Repeats assess variation but do not automatically remove a consistent delay in the chosen technique.',
'Describe a consistent start event for timing repeated pendulum oscillations.','Start timing as the bob crosses a marked reference point in a stated direction.')
add('2.1-04','Instrument choice involves trade-offs in resolution, range and the physical access needed.','A smaller scale increment is useful only when the instrument can correctly measure the intended surfaces or interval.',
'A measuring tape covers a long distance conveniently but may sag. A micrometer gives fine external readings but has a limited opening and cannot replace internal caliper jaws. A stopwatch provides fine displayed increments, yet reaction timing may dominate a short event. Explain an advantage or limitation in relation to the actual measurement. A tool is not best simply because it displays more digits; suitability depends on the experiment.',
'Explain why a measuring tape can be preferable to a micrometer for the length of a classroom bench.','The tape has a range suited to the bench length.~The micrometer’s limited opening and external measuring geometry do not suit that long measurement.')
add('2.1-05','A period is the time for one complete repeated cycle.','Timing several cycles and dividing by their count estimates the period while reducing the relative influence of start/stop delays.',
'For a pendulum, a complete oscillation returns to the same position and direction. A crossing in the opposite direction after half a cycle should not be counted as a full cycle. If 15 oscillations take 24.0 s, the estimated period is 1.60 s. Repeat the measurement under the same conditions to judge variation. Keep the length and release conditions fixed when the aim is to estimate one period rather than investigate how it changes.',
'Describe how to obtain a pendulum period from a stopwatch timing of many cycles.','Time a known number of complete oscillations and divide the elapsed time by that number.')
add('2.1-06','For a simple pendulum under small-angle school conditions at one location, its period depends on its length rather than its bob mass.','A controlled test changes one candidate factor while keeping the others appropriately fixed.',
'To test length, measure from the pivot to the bob’s centre, choose several lengths and obtain a mean period at each. Keep bob mass and a small release angle matched. To test mass separately, change mass while retaining length and release angle. A large amplitude or changed gravitational field would invalidate the simple statement that length is the sole relevant factor. Within the stated conditions, compare periods using the measurement quality rather than declaring an effect from tiny inconsistent differences.',
'Describe a controlled change that tests whether pendulum length affects its period.','Change the pivot-to-bob-centre length and compare repeated period estimates while keeping bob mass and small release angle matched.')
add('2.2-01','A scalar is a quantity described by magnitude without direction.','Mass, distance, speed, energy and time are scalar quantities.',
'A mass of 2 kg does not need a compass direction to specify it. A scalar can still have a unit, uncertainty or a value that changes with time. Calling it scalar does not mean it is constant. When comparing distance with displacement, remember that distance describes the path length and remains a scalar even when the path bends. Magnitude gives the amount; it does not by itself say that a quantity is a vector.',
'Define a scalar quantity.','A scalar has magnitude but no associated direction.')
add('2.2-02','A vector is a quantity requiring magnitude and direction.','Force, displacement, velocity and acceleration are vector quantities.',
'A force of 5 N east differs from a force of 5 N west even though their magnitudes match. An arrow can represent the vector: a chosen scale gives its magnitude and the arrowhead shows direction. Reversing the arrow reverses the vector, rather than making its magnitude a different size. In one-dimensional calculations, a sign convention can encode direction, but the positive direction must first be stated.',
'Define a vector quantity.','A vector requires both magnitude and direction.')
add('2.2-03','The scalar/vector distinction concerns whether direction is part of the quantity.','Speed is scalar whereas velocity includes direction; distance is scalar whereas displacement includes the start-to-finish direction.',
'Two vehicles can have equal speeds while travelling in opposite directions, so their velocities differ. Similarly, an object that returns to its start can travel a nonzero distance but have zero net displacement. A vector addition therefore cannot generally be replaced by adding magnitudes. Specify examples with enough information to reveal the distinction. A unit such as m/s can be shared by speed and velocity without removing the need to state velocity direction.',
'Explain the difference between two objects having the same speed and having the same velocity.','Equal speeds require equal magnitudes of motion.~Equal velocities additionally require the same direction.')
add('2.2-04','Perpendicular vector components form a right-angled triangle with their resultant.','Pythagoras gives the resultant magnitude and a trigonometric ratio gives its direction.',
'For components 8 N east and 6 N north, the resultant magnitude is 10 N. Its direction is approximately 36.9° north of east because tan θ is northward component divided by eastward component. Identify the reference direction in the answer: the complementary angle would be measured from north. Adding 8 and 6 to obtain 14 N would ignore the perpendicular directions. Keep the units and the arrow direction when drawing the corresponding triangle.',
'Calculate the magnitude and direction of the resultant of 4 N east and 3 N north. Give the angle north of east.','Magnitude = √(4² + 3²) = 5 N.~The direction is tan⁻¹(3/4) = 36.9° north of east.',guidance=['Equation and working for the resultant magnitude: Pythagoras gives 5 N (1 mark).','Final directional answer from the component ratio: 36.9° north of east (1 mark).'])
add('2.2-05','Vectors at an arbitrary angle can be added with a scale drawing using a tip-to-tail triangle or a parallelogram.','The resultant runs from the starting tail to the final tip, not along the remaining side in the reverse direction.',
'Choose and state a length scale, such as 1 cm for 1 N. Draw the first vector at its direction, then move the second vector parallel to itself so that its tail meets the first tip. Its size and direction are unchanged by this translation. Join the original start to the final tip with an arrow and measure the resultant’s scaled length and direction. In a parallelogram construction, both component tails share an origin and the resultant is the diagonal to the opposite corner.',
'Draw a tip-to-tail scale diagram adding 3 N east to 3 N at 60° north of east. Indicate the resultant.','Join the two correctly directed 3 N vectors tip to tail.~Draw the resultant arrow from the original start to the final tip; it is about 5.20 N at 30° north of east.')
A.vector('2.2-04',[(4,0),(3,90)],title='Perpendicular components and resultant')
A.vector('2.2-05',[(3,0),(3,60)],title='Tip-to-tail addition at an arbitrary angle')
C=A.choice
C('2.1-01-M02','Which instrument is calibrated for a liquid volume?',('Measuring cylinder','Its graduations report volume.'),('Stopwatch','It measures time.'),('Ammeter','It measures current.'),('Metre rule','It directly measures length.'))
C('2.1-02-M02','Which caliper feature measures the inside width of an opening?',('Internal jaws','They contact the inner surfaces.'),('Outside jaws only','Those enclose an outer dimension.'),('The zero-checking label','This is not a measuring contact.'),('An external micrometer anvil','It cannot span the inside opening in the usual external arrangement.'))
C('2.1-02-M03','Why is a micrometer ratchet useful?',('It helps limit measuring force','This reduces excessive squeezing of the sample.'),('It removes all zero error','A ratchet does not set every scale zero.'),('It changes a diameter into a radius','That is a separate geometrical calculation.'),('It measures internal volume','A micrometer is not a volume vessel.'))
C('2.1-05','What counts as one full pendulum oscillation?',('Return to the same position and direction','Both position and direction identify a completed cycle.'),('Any crossing of the centre','The opposite-direction crossing occurs after half a cycle.'),('Travel from one extreme to the other','This is half a cycle.'),('Any visible movement','The interval must be defined.'))
C('2.2-01','Which quantity is scalar?',('Energy','It has magnitude without a direction.'),('Force','Force is a vector.'),('Velocity','Velocity includes direction.'),('Displacement','Displacement includes direction.'))
C('2.2-02','What additional information distinguishes a vector from a scalar magnitude?',('Direction','Direction is part of the vector specification.'),('Whether a unit exists','Scalars can have units too.'),('Whether it changes','Either kind can change.'),('Whether it is measured experimentally','Both kinds can be measured.'))
C('2.2-05','In a tip-to-tail construction, where does the resultant begin?',('At the first vector’s original tail','It connects the overall start to the final tip.'),('At the final tip, pointing back','That gives the reversed resultant.'),('At the midpoint of either component','The overall endpoints determine the sum.'),('At a new unrelated origin','The construction must retain the combined displacement.'))
C('2.1-01-M01','A rod extends from 2.0 cm to 10.7 cm on a ruler. What is its length?',('8.7 cm','Subtract the starting position.'),('10.7 cm','This assumes a zero start.'),('12.7 cm','This adds the endpoint positions.'),('5.35 cm','Halving one endpoint has no measurement basis.'))
C('2.1-02-M02','A 0.1 mm vernier gives 12 mm on the main scale and alignment at division 4. With no zero error, what is the reading?',('12.4 mm','Add four times 0.1 mm to 12 mm.'),('16 mm','This treats divisions as millimetres.'),('12.04 mm','This assumes a 0.01 mm resolution.'),('11.6 mm','There is no stated correction requiring subtraction.'))
C('2.1-02-M03','A micrometer sleeve shows 4.5 mm and the thimble adds 0.18 mm. With no zero error, what is the result?',('4.68 mm','Add the two scale contributions.'),('4.18 mm','This misses the half-millimetre sleeve value.'),('4.32 mm','This subtracts instead of adding.'),('18.45 mm','This misreads the scales as decimal strings.'))
C('2.1-05','Twenty complete cycles take 32.0 s. What is the period?',('1.60 s','Divide the elapsed time by twenty.'),('640 s','This multiplies instead of dividing.'),('0.625 s','This reverses the quotient.'),('32.0 s','This is the time of the whole sequence.'))
C('2.2-04','Two perpendicular forces have magnitudes 5 N and 12 N. What is the resultant magnitude?',('13 N','Pythagoras gives the right-triangle resultant.'),('17 N','This would add collinear same-direction magnitudes.'),('7 N','This subtracts magnitudes without the stated directions.'),('169 N','This omits the square root and has the wrong dimensional interpretation.'))
P=A.part
P('2.2-01','State whether mass is a scalar or a vector.','Mass is a scalar.',1,'One mark for scalar.')
P('2.1-05','State what is meant by one complete oscillation.','A return to the same position and direction in the repeated motion.',1,'One mark for a complete cycle rather than a half-cycle.')
P('2.1-05','Explain why timing 25 oscillations can give a better period estimate than timing one.','Starting and stopping delays contribute a smaller fraction of the longer timed interval; dividing by 25 estimates one period.',2,'One mark for the longer interval reducing relative reaction-time influence; one for division by the correct cycle count.')
P('2.1-05','Calculate the period if 25 oscillations take 37.5 s.','The period is 37.5/25 = 1.50 s.',2,'One mark for dividing by 25; one for 1.50 s.')
P('2.2-04','Calculate the resultant magnitude of perpendicular displacements 9 m east and 12 m north.','The magnitude is √(9² + 12²) = 15 m.',2,'One mark for the squared-component relation; one for 15 m.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot oscillation count horizontally and elapsed time vertically. The idealised straight trend passes through the origin.',unit='s per oscillation',meaning='The gradient gives the period of this fixed-length pendulum because it expresses elapsed time per complete cycle.',method=['Suspend a bob from a securely supported string and measure its length from pivot to bob centre.','Choose a small fixed release angle and define a reference crossing and direction for counting cycles.','Time a chosen number of complete oscillations with a stopwatch.','Repeat that count and calculate a mean elapsed time, investigating an anomalous timing.','Repeat for several counts with the same length and release conditions.'],control='Keep pivot-to-centre length and small release angle fixed; avoid pushing the bob and keep its swing clear of people.',table='Record cycle count and each elapsed time / s, with a mean-time column after repeats.',conclusion='Plot mean elapsed time against cycle count. Use the gradient to estimate a period and the scatter to judge consistency; this is a period measurement, not a test of length dependence.')
